# Logitech G815 (046d:c33f), cabled, its HID++ interface 01 -> uaccess for
# the seat user. The G813 has the same id. Not yet checked on this hardware:
# the interface is from Solaar's device descriptor (interface 1), Solaar's
# logs of the keyboard's node and a published lsusb of it, read 2026-10-18.
# The keyboard has no receiver, so its USB identity is its own and the
# property match below is scoped by interface, as the G915's is.
#
# This node carries what is typed on the G815. Interface 01's report
# descriptor holds the keyboard's own key array, a mouse report and its
# media keys beside HID++'s long reports (a published report descriptor;
# libratbag's log of one shows key reports arriving on the node), so a
# program the seat's user runs can read what is typed on the keyboard while
# this rule is installed. That is a wider exposure than the G502 HERO's
# interface 01, which carries only what its buttons are bound to type.
# Gearbench drops those reports unread. Granted through uaccess, the owner's
# decision of 2026-10-18. Interface 00, the boot keyboard, gets nothing.
#
# The grant crosses accounts, as 70-gearbench-uinput.rules says of
# /dev/uinput's: a program that opened the node keeps reading it after another
# account gets the seat, and so reads what that account types on the G815, a
# password at the login screen included. On a machine several people use, that
# file's group grant, in a copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/, limits the
# node to the group's accounts. A member then reads it from any session,
# whoever has the seat.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c33f", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="01", TAG+="uaccess"
